<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!-- Pairing reads a QR code off the desktop's screen. Requested at the
         moment the camera is opened and not at install, and the app works
         without it: a pairing payload can be pasted instead. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CAMERA" />
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.camera.any" android:required="false" />

    <!-- The LAN leg is a direct TCP connection to the desktop; the relay leg
         is an outbound WebSocket. Both need the network and nothing else. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />

    <!-- An agent waiting for you is the whole point of carrying this app, so
         the notification is not decoration. Asked at the moment the Agent
         Center first connects rather than at install, and the app works
         without it: the same alerts are on the Agent Center's own list.

         Since P1-058 there are two ways one arrives. The poll loop raises them
         while the app is open; a UnifiedPush distributor delivers them when it
         is not, with the screen off and this app's network suspended in Doze.
         The second needs a distributor installed on this phone — ntfy, for
         instance — and without one the app behaves exactly as it did before:
         alerts while it is open, and none while it is closed. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

    <!-- Keeping this app from drifting away from the machines it talks to.

         This phone is not running APEX-OS. The APK is in no image and `apex
         update` cannot reach it, so unlike the ChatGPT and Claude DESKTOP apps
         — which must not self-update, because `sudo apex update` already moves
         them — there is no other path here. Without this permission the app
         only ever moves forward when its owner remembers to go and look, and a
         client that falls behind the OS is the exact failure the protocol
         window on the other side of this app exists to survive.

         What it buys is narrow and worth stating, because the name sounds
         broader than it is. It does NOT let this app install anything quietly,
         and it does not let it install anything else: it earns the right to
         ASK, and Android still draws its own "do you want to update this app?"
         screen, which the user can refuse. GrapheneOS draws it too. The user
         can also revoke it afterwards from Settings and the app keeps working
         — the update row simply offers to send them there again.

         The APK never becomes a file this app owns. It is streamed from the
         release straight into a `PackageInstaller` session while its SHA-256
         is computed, and the session is committed only if the digest matches
         the one the release published — so there is no download directory, no
         FileProvider, no content:// URI, and nothing for
         `InsecureStorageTest`'s byte-by-byte walk of app storage to account
         for. `ManifestTest` asserts that the permission list is exactly these
         five and no more. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.REQUEST_INSTALL_PACKAGES" />

    <!-- Push-to-talk, and what is NOT here is the point of it.

         RECORD_AUDIO is absent, deliberately. Speech reaches this app through
         `RecognizerIntent.ACTION_RECOGNIZE_SPEECH`: the system's own recogniser
         opens, shows the system's own microphone indicator, and hands back
         text. No audio ever enters this process, so there is no microphone
         permission to grant, to forget about, or to be abused if this app is
         ever compromised — and this is the app that shows which machine is
         about to run something as root.

         That is the same rule P1-059's fourth criterion states for the photo
         library, applied to the microphone: take the Android-mediated
         selection, never the blanket permission. Which is also why no storage
         or media permission appears anywhere in this file; `ManifestTest`
         asserts their absence rather than trusting it, because the moment a
         file-handoff transport exists the obvious way to read the file is a
         permission.

         This `<queries>` entry is what package visibility on API 30 and later
         requires for the recogniser to be visible at all. Without it,
         `resolveActivity` answers null on a phone that HAS a recogniser, which
         is refusal and absence confused in the direction that makes a working
         feature look broken. -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.speech.action.RECOGNIZE_SPEECH" />
        </intent>
        <!-- The photo picker's backport, for P1-059's file handoff.

             `ActivityResultContracts.PickVisualMedia` prefers
             `MediaStore.ACTION_PICK_IMAGES` where the platform has it and
             otherwise looks for a system fallback picker, which it finds with
             `PackageManager.resolveActivity` on this action — read out of
             activity-1.12.4's own bytecode, not assumed. Package visibility
             filters that call on API 30 and later, and a null answer sends the
             contract down the `ACTION_OPEN_DOCUMENT` path instead: not broken,
             but not the camera roll a person expects when they press Photo.

             Declaring it costs nothing and is the same fix the recogniser
             above needed. NOT VERIFIED ON A DEVICE — there is no device and no
             emulator here, so what is asserted is that the declaration is
             present, and `ManifestTest` is where that assertion lives. -->
        <intent>
            <action android:name="androidx.activity.result.contract.action.PICK_IMAGES" />
        </intent>
        <!-- Opening any other file goes through the Storage Access Framework,
             which needs no <queries> entry: `startActivity` is not filtered by
             package visibility and the contract resolves nothing. Said here so
             the absence is a decision and not an oversight. -->

        <!-- Finding the UnifiedPush distributors installed on this phone
             (P1-058). A distributor is identified by the receiver it declares
             for this action, and package visibility on API 30 and later
             filters `queryBroadcastReceivers` — so without this entry the
             answer is an empty list on a phone that HAS a distributor, which
             is refusal and absence confused in the direction that makes a
             working feature look broken. Exactly the failure the recogniser
             entry above exists to avoid. -->
        <intent>
            <action android:name="org.unifiedpush.android.distributor.REGISTER" />
        </intent>
    </queries>

    <application
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:fullBackupContent="false"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.ApexRemote">

        <!--
          `allowBackup` is false and the extraction rules deny everything, on
          purpose and not out of caution. A backup of this app's files would
          copy the sealed device key to wherever the backup goes, and the
          keystore key that unseals it does NOT travel — so the copy is useless
          to its owner and is one more place the ciphertext exists for everyone
          else. Re-pairing takes ten seconds and produces a fresh key; that is
          the right recovery story.
        -->

        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:configChanges="orientation|screenSize|screenLayout|keyboardHidden|uiMode"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>

            <!-- A pairing payload opened from anywhere else: a camera app that
                 resolves URIs, a message, a paste. The scheme is what makes it
                 reach this app instead of a browser. -->
            <intent-filter android:autoVerify="false">
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
                <data android:scheme="apex-remote" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!-- Where `PackageInstaller` answers, and where the user's install
             prompt is actually raised: `commit()` shows nothing by itself, it
             broadcasts STATUS_PENDING_USER_ACTION carrying the intent that
             draws the prompt.

             Declared here rather than registered at runtime because the status
             can arrive after this process has gone, and `exported="false"`
             because the only sender that should ever reach it is the system
             replying to a session this app created. -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".update.UpdateInstallReceiver"
            android:exported="false">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="com.apexos.remote.UPDATE_STATUS" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>

        <!-- Where a UnifiedPush distributor delivers (P1-058).

             `exported="true"` is required and is not a hole. A distributor is
             a separate app the user installed; it must be able to broadcast to
             this receiver, and the UnifiedPush specification is built on
             exactly that. What stops a hostile app abusing it is not the
             manifest — it is that a message is only acted on if it DECRYPTS
             under a key one paired machine holds and nothing else does, and
             that its sequence number is higher than the last one seen. An app
             that sends this receiver a broadcast gets nothing: no
             notification, no state change, no answer.

             All four connector actions are declared. Missing one fails
             SILENTLY — the distributor simply never delivers that message and
             nothing on the phone says so — which is why `ManifestTest` asserts
             the set rather than trusting it. -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".push.PushReceiver"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="org.unifiedpush.android.connector.MESSAGE" />
                <action android:name="org.unifiedpush.android.connector.NEW_ENDPOINT" />
                <action android:name="org.unifiedpush.android.connector.REGISTRATION_FAILED" />
                <action android:name="org.unifiedpush.android.connector.UNREGISTERED" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>
    </application>
</manifest>
